This is the complete routine for the Main Health thread: reading CI, the two scheduled checks that stand in for it, their shared setup, which tier runs which part, and how one thread hands off to the next.
It lives in the repo, not only in project instructions or a working file, so it survives losing either; read this file first on every rotation rather than relying on an earlier thread's memory of it.
`.forge/main-health-patterns.md` says what this thread watches and when it escalates; this file says how.

## Why two routines exist

This repo runs no CI on pull requests or on push to `main`: every check workflow under `.github/workflows/` is manual-dispatch only, to keep CI minutes down (`.forge/ci-and-checks.md`, "Don't manually trigger CI runs").
The full check pass is dispatched on `main` on the owner's schedule (twice a day by default), only when something has merged since the last pass, so between dispatches a regression on `main` has no CI signal at all.

**Dispatching the real workflows is always the preferred signal.**
The local full-suite script (Routine 2) exists only because CI runners aren't affordable more often than the scheduled dispatch: it is a local stand-in for the missing signal, a workaround, not a replacement for real CI.
If CI becomes cheap enough to run more often (a plan change, a self-hosted runner), dispatch real CI more often and retire or shrink this script's role.
Nothing here changes the "Don't manually trigger CI runs" rule: this thread still never dispatches or re-runs a workflow outside that rule's two named exceptions.

The other routine, the **post-merge review pass**, is not a stand-in for anything.
It reviews what several merged PRs did *together*, catching a class of bug (cross-PR, migration, resource-exhaustion-under-execution) that even fully staffed per-PR CI and review wouldn't, so it runs on its own merit regardless of CI affordability.

## Shared setup: pay the environment cost once per thread

Both routines need the same environment: the project's dependencies, any local services the full suite needs (a database, a message broker, a browser for end-to-end tests), and the pre-commit hook.
Set it up once when the thread starts, not once per routine firing:

```
{{ setup_command }}
```

Make the setup idempotent, so re-running it on a later firing just confirms everything is still there (`docs/agents/build-thread-setup.md` has the per-scope detail if something's missing).
Keep the thread's starting worktree rather than re-cloning or re-fetching into a fresh one per firing: both routines read `origin/main`, so `git fetch origin main` in place is enough.

## Operational safety rules

Both routines, and every review sub-agent either dispatches, follow `.agents/skills/post-merge-review/SKILL.md`'s "Operational safety rules"; read them before running either routine, not only before a post-merge review pass.

## Routine 1: the post-merge review pass

**Cadence**: every 4 hours, skipping the firing if nothing has merged to `main` (or to any upstream repo the tracker says the pass also covers) since the watermark.
Schedule it with the harness's recurring-trigger tool, bound to this session (for example cron `0 2,6,10,14,18,22 * * *`), and have the fired prompt check the watermark first: a no-op firing shouldn't run the setup or spin up reviewers.
The hours are offset from Routine 2's passes so the two seldom meet; when they do, Routine 2's overlap rule decides which yields.
Every 5th pass also includes `post-merge-review`'s step 5a (the tautological-test sample), which follows the overlap rule and never runs without the owner's yes on that pass's decision card.

**Procedure**: `.agents/skills/post-merge-review/SKILL.md` has the full procedure (the watermark format, the surviving-lines file set, batching by subsystem, the grading rules, the **security axis** (STRIDE against `docs/security/threat-model.md` and the rest of `docs/security/`, with no change to this cadence), the capability-tier split, filing and bundling, and the operational safety rules); read it before running a pass rather than relying on a summary here.
The tracker issue {{ pmr_ref }} holds the pending notes (one comment per merged PR), the watermark and the run log (SDLC-095), never the procedure: an issue body has a size limit and is the wrong place for text that changes more often than the skill enforcing it.

**The same firing also runs `queue-sweep`** (`.agents/skills/queue-sweep/SKILL.md`): the first firing of each day (02:00 UTC in the example cron), so no separate trigger or state file picks which firing runs it.
Unlike the post-merge review pass, it runs every time that hour comes round, with no watermark skip, since its gaps build up from comments and label changes as well as merges.
It is tracker API reads only, with no test load, so it needs no environment setup and the Routine 2 overlap rule doesn't apply; reports land in the working folder's `queue-sweep/`.

### Forge drift check

**The same first daily firing also runs the forge drift check** as its own step (`queue-sweep` doesn't call it).
It is API and git only, with no test load, so it needs no environment setup and stays outside the Routine 2 overlap rule.
`behind` counts only commits touching the upstream's template subdirectory or its Copier config file (`render_pathspec` in `check_forge_drift.py`), so a commit changing only this repository's own ADRs or design docs never moves it.
`behind` is counted against the ref a default sync would target (the `target=` field: the latest release tag, or HEAD when upstream has no tag); `unreleased=<n>`, shown only when tags exist, counts commits past that tag for information, never as drift.

1. Run `python3 scripts/check_forge_drift.py`.
2. **Exit 3 (`entrypoint=missing`):** report it to the owner immediately as a Must, "the forge rules are not loading", whatever the drift result and before the steps below.
3. **Behind (`behind=<n>` with `n > 0` on the result line, whatever the exit code, so a missing entrypoint doesn't suppress the ticket).**
   Look for an open issue whose title starts with `Sync forge-owned files to TheForge ` **and whose author is the held sync ticket's trusted author** (the per-repo value above): the one identity Main Health and `forge-sync` file or act on this ticket under, which another account can't forge whatever label or body it gives a look-alike.
   - If it carries `needs-decision`, retitle it to the new short upstream SHA and replace its body with the new result.
   - If it exists without `needs-decision`, the owner has released it and a sync is under way: leave it alone and only mention the drift in the report, since it is now the running sync's backing ticket.
   - If there is none from that author, create it: title `Sync forge-owned files to TheForge <short upstream sha>`, label `needs-decision` and only that, body the result line, the subject list (ending "…and N more" when `behind` exceeds the listed subjects), and the sentence "Held until the owner says to sync; then run `.agents/skills/forge-sync/SKILL.md` against this ticket."
     A title-matching issue from a different author is never retitled, never treated as released, and never counted as "none" above: it doesn't block filing or updating the real ticket; mention it once in the daily report as a possible forged look-alike.
   - Never label it `queued`, and never run `forge-sync`.
4. **Current (exit 0):** nothing to do.
5. **Unknown (exit 2):** mention the error once in the daily report; file nothing.

## Routine 2: the local full-suite stand-in check

**The script is project-owned and not generated.**
If the project has not yet written `{{ local_full_suite_script }}`, this routine is an open gap: record it on the post-merge review tracker {{ pmr_ref }} and run only Routine 1 and the scheduled CI pass.
Lead that note with its own marker, `<!-- local-full-suite-script-missing -->`, distinct from the pending-note and archive-comment markers on the same tracker, so `queue-sweep` check 11(a) never mistakes it for either.
The script runs every check that can run in the agent's sandbox (lint, type checks, the pin guard, migrations, the full test suite with its coverage floor, end-to-end tests against local services) as named steps, and itself:

- derives its pre-commit-backed steps from `.pre-commit-config.yaml`'s own hook ids (one step per hook, skipping only ones meaningless on a checkout of `main`, such as a branch-name check) rather than hand-copying a second list, so a hook added to the config also runs against `main` with no edit here;
- holds an exclusive lock for its whole run (for example `flock` on a file at one fixed path, inside a directory the script creates private to its user and refuses when another account owns it or could write to it), exits with a distinct code (75 by convention) if a second run starts, and offers `--is-running` (exit 0 means running);
- accepts a `SKIP` list of step names for a resumed or partial run;
- **on a full run (`SKIP` empty), and only when an explicit publish switch is set** (for example `{{ local_full_suite_publish_var }}=1`), **posts one comment on the local full-suite evidence tracker {{ lfs_ref }}**: a single-line marker `<!-- local-full-suite-evidence sha=<sha> finished=<ISO8601 UTC> aggregate=pass|fail -->` (`pass` only when every step passed) followed by a human-readable per-step table.
  This comment is the run's canonical evidence (SDLC-099) in every environment this routine targets, including a sandbox whose GitHub proxy refuses commit-status writes, and it must succeed: log `ISSUE-POST-FAILED` and treat it as a script bug, never as a reason to discard the run's actual result;
- **also attempts a commit status per step it ran** (`{{ lfs_status_context }}/<step>`, success or failure, a description like `PASS 312s`) plus one aggregate `{{ lfs_status_context }}` status, all on the run's HEAD sha, as a best-effort secondary record gated by the same publish switch.
  Some agent environments refuse commit-status writes outright; that refusal is expected there, not a bug: log `STATUS-POST-REFUSED` and carry on.
  A status post failing for any other reason logs `STATUS-POST-FAILED`, which *is* worth investigating, since the tracker comment already proves the run and its publish switch worked;
- on a fully clean run with publishing on, first re-checks that the run's sha is still `origin/main`'s current head (a fresh `git fetch origin main && git rev-parse origin/main`, since `main` can move during a long run): if not, it skips the push and logs `GREEN-PUSH-SKIPPED-STALE`, which is no failure, because a clean result for an older sha isn't evidence to push over a newer, unverified one.
  Otherwise it fast-forwards `refs/heads/green` to that commit with a plain non-force push (`git push origin <sha>:refs/heads/green`), which needs only `git push`, not the statuses.
  A rejection the remote makes because of the ref's own state (non-fast-forward) or a platform policy that applies regardless (such as GitHub's GH007 private-email-address block, where it applies) is logged as `GREEN-PUSH-REFUSED`, never a script bug: `green` is stuck and needs the recovery below, not a code fix.
  A rejection for bad or expired credentials (a principal with no write access, a token error) is logged as `GREEN-PUSH-FAILED`: an environment misconfiguration to fix, not a stuck ref.
  Any other push failure (a local git error, a network failure) is also `GREEN-PUSH-FAILED`, a script bug worth investigating.

**Cadence**: on a schedule (twice daily by default, interleaved with the dispatched-CI cadence so the two signals alternate), skipping the firing if `origin/main`'s head hasn't moved since the commit in `last-verified.txt` in the working folder's `main-health/` directory.
Schedule it with the harness's recurring-trigger tool, and have the fired prompt do that skip check (`git fetch origin main && git rev-parse origin/main` against `last-verified.txt`) before running anything.

**Overlap rule**: the two routines never run at the same time, because the combined load makes timing-sensitive tests time out.
A full-suite firing that finds Routine 1's review sub-agents still running waits for their completion notifications before starting (no sleep or poll loop).
A Routine 1 firing that finds the full suite running is skipped; its watermark check picks the same commits up at the next firing.
The script's own lock enforces the full-suite side, and a Routine 1 firing checks `{{ local_full_suite_script }} --is-running` before it starts.

**Running it**:

```
{{ local_full_suite_publish_var }}=1 {{ local_full_suite_script }} <worktree> <log-dir>
```

The publish switch turns on the real tracker-comment and commit-status posts and the `green` push; this routine's own invocation is the one place that sets it, and nothing else should, including a manual run you're using to debug the script itself.
The script in the repo is the canonical copy; don't keep a second copy in the working folder or anywhere else.

**Known measurement quirks**: keep the running list in the Main Health patterns notes file (`.forge/main-health-patterns.md`, "Patterns worth recording"), not here.
Two belong in every project's list from the start: run the full suite the same way real CI runs it (same parallelism, same environment variables), or a pass here doesn't mean a pass there; and don't run review sub-agents concurrently with a full-suite pass.

**A dependency-audit step failure is a finding, not a flake.**
An audit step (a full-tree vulnerability audit of the pinned dependencies) can go red with no merge behind it, because a new advisory was published against a pin already here; until cleared, it holds back the `green` push like any other failing step.
Don't re-run it, and don't spend the one allowed re-run on it: read the audit's output for the package and advisory, then either file a version-bump ticket (bug-first, since this is a live vulnerability) when a fix exists, or, when none does yet, add a reasoned, tracked entry to the project's advisory-ignore list through a normal ticket and PR under the same review gate.
Report it like any other red step.

**Every full run (`SKIP` empty) posts its own evidence (SDLC-099, SDLC-103)** to the local full-suite evidence tracker {{ lfs_ref }}, with no separate action here.
A resumed or partial run (`SKIP` non-empty) attempts per-step commit statuses for the steps it reran but posts no tracker comment and no aggregate status: a run that didn't exercise every check has nothing to aggregate and is not the record `queue-sweep`'s overdue check looks for.
Treat an `ISSUE-POST-FAILED`, `STATUS-POST-FAILED` or `GREEN-PUSH-FAILED` log line as a script bug to investigate, never as a reason to treat the run's actual result as unproven, and never as a reason to force-push.
`STATUS-POST-REFUSED` (where the environment's GitHub proxy blocks commit-status writes; the tracker comment is the evidence there) and `GREEN-PUSH-SKIPPED-STALE` (`origin/main` moved during the run) need no action.
`GREEN-PUSH-REFUSED` means `green` is stuck at its current value and no later push will fast-forward past it on its own: report it and follow "Recovery when the green push is refused" below, rather than treating it as a script bug or retrying the push.

**Recovery when the green push is refused.** A `GREEN-PUSH-REFUSED` line means the remote declined a plain non-force push, for one of two reasons: `refs/heads/green` points somewhere that isn't an ancestor of the pushed sha (a true non-fast-forward, which this routine's refusal to force-push leaves permanently stuck, since no later `main` commit can fast-forward past it either), or a platform policy blocked the push regardless of ancestry (such as GitHub's GH007 private-email-address block, where it applies).
Report it to the owner rather than resolving it yourself: ask them to delete `refs/heads/green` in the GitHub web UI.
Once it's gone, recreate it through the REST API's create-ref call (`gh api repos/<owner>/<repo>/git/refs -f ref=refs/heads/green -f sha=<verified head sha>`), after confirming that sha both equals `origin/main`'s current head and was the sha of a run that passed clean.
A REST ref update with `force=false` (`gh api --method PATCH repos/<owner>/<repo>/git/refs/heads/green -f sha=<sha>`) is fast-forward-only, like the push that just failed, so it can replace the delete-and-recreate only for a policy-block refusal, not for a true non-fast-forward, whose ancestry problem fails the same way over any transport.
Where it applies and the environment's token and proxy allow a ref write (some agent proxies refuse ref writes, which is why the web-UI delete is the fallback that always works), it saves the delete step.

**On a clean pass**: update `last-verified.txt` to the commit SHA that just passed.
**On a failure**: leave `last-verified.txt` where it was, so the next firing retries from the same baseline, not a newer unverified one.
Treat it like a dispatched-CI failure, using "Reading the scheduled CI pass" below: find the culprit commit and PR, rule out infrastructure noise, root-cause it, and report.

**When a real CI run is available**: a green dispatched run of the full check pass on `origin/main`'s current head is strictly stronger evidence than a local pass on the same commit.
It also sets `last-verified.txt` to that head, so Routine 2's skip check skips that commit.
After confirming the run is green, fast-forward `refs/heads/green` to that head by hand (`git push origin <head>:refs/heads/green`, no `--force`), since the dispatched workflow doesn't push it.
This only says what to do with a green result once one lands; it changes nothing about *triggering* real CI.

## Reading the scheduled CI pass

When the owner's scheduled full-pass routine fires real CI workflows, read the result this way.

1. **List recent runs on `main`.**
   Re-check the dispatched-workflow list in "Per-repo values" against `.github/workflows/` itself, since it can grow.
   A lint workflow is manual-dispatch only and not part of the scheduled pass (the pre-commit hook is the real lint gate); workflows needing resources the sandbox lacks, or making paid external calls, are out of scope.
   For each in-scope workflow, get the most recent completed run on branch `main` (through the hosting platform's API or its MCP tools: workflow runs filtered to branch `main` and status `completed`, one per page), verifying the tool names against the live toolset at the start of a rotation, since they can be renamed.
   A workflow the last pass didn't dispatch may not have run recently; absence isn't failure, so check only ones that *have* a recent run.
2. **Any failed conclusion?**
   If every latest run succeeded, or nothing is new since the last check, stop here.
   - **2a. A timeout is a real failure to profile, not a limit to raise.**
     Profile first (find the hotspot, fix or justify it) before proposing a new limit.
     Raising a timeout without profiling needs the owner's explicit sign-off each time, and timing out again after one raise means profile again, not ask for more.
3. **Rule out infrastructure noise before treating it as a real break.**
   Check the run's billable time and whether its job logs have content: a run that billed about nothing with no logs is a runner-provisioning blip.
   For infrastructure noise, re-run the failed jobs once; if the re-run is still infrastructure-shaped, note it and stop.
4. **Find the commit and the PR that merged it.**
   The failing run's head SHA is the `main` commit to blame; get its message, or for a squash merge resolve it to its PR with `scripts/detect_pr_for_commit.py {{ repo_owner }}/{{ repo_name }} <sha>` (a `merge_commit_sha` lookup) rather than searching merged PRs by title or timestamp, which misses a custom squash title.
   - **4a. A red loop before a hypothesis.**
     Follow `diagnosing-bugs` Phase 1: one command, run locally, that goes red on the reported symptom, before any theory about the cause.
5. **Idempotency.**
   Before investigating further, check the culprit PR's comments for a marker containing this run's id (`<!-- watch-main-ci:run-<run_id> -->`); if present, stop.
6. **Investigate root cause.**
   Pull the failing job's logs (failed jobs only).
   Check whether the failure is inside the diff the culprit PR touched; a failure elsewhere is more likely pre-existing flakiness surfacing than something that PR broke.
   Re-run once before concluding "flaky" from a single unreproduced failure.
7. **Report.**
   Post one comment on the culprit PR: which check failed, the actual error, whether it's inside or outside that PR's own diff, and a proposed fix only if it's a small, confident, low-risk one-liner.
   Include the `<!-- watch-main-ci:run-<run_id> -->` marker.
   Never push directly to `main` to fix a break; open a small ticketed PR if you're confident in a fix, otherwise the report is the deliverable.
8. **Also surface it to whoever is watching this session directly**: a broken `main` outranks most things a background loop stays quiet about.

