## Starting this thread

Before anything else, arm both Routine 1's and Routine 2's triggers with the harness's recurring-trigger tool, bound to this session, using the cadence and skip logic above.
This applies identically to a brand-new Main Health thread and to one rotating in from a handover: don't wait for "Rotation and handover" below, or infer from its mention there that trigger creation is only a hand-off step.
A fresh trigger's id won't match any predecessor's; that is normal.

## Rotation and handover

**Default trigger**: the Main Health thread rotates at a pass boundary (after a completed post-merge review run or full-suite pass), not on a fixed run count or context percentage; the build-thread rotation rule in `.forge/coordinator-core.md` (4 tickets or about 50% context) doesn't apply here.
This is a **Default** (`.forge/never-default.md`): the coordinator or the owner can call for a different point when the work genuinely needs it (for example mid-investigation of a real break), and the thread then says so explicitly rather than silently drifting past it.
Rotating after several post-merge review runs per thread, rather than every run, is deliberate: a thread that has seen several can spot a pattern across them and turn it into a self-review recommendation.

**`handover.md` carries state only.**
It is a rotation-scoped snapshot of mutable facts (heads, watermarks, trigger ids, known failures, open items), never where a procedure note, a lesson or anything meant to outlive this rotation is first written.
Such a note belongs in this skill, the Main Health patterns notes file, `.agents/skills/diagnosing-bugs/SKILL.md` or `CLAUDE.md`'s project-owned sections, whichever fits, **landed in the repo before the handover is written**.
A `handover.md` carrying a procedure note instead of (or as well as) a repo edit is not state-only, and the incoming thread treats that as a gap to close, not a quirk to tolerate.

**When you are the outgoing thread**:

1. Land every procedure note or lesson from this rotation in the repo first (a small ticketed doc or skill PR; `Requirements: none (internal)`), before step 2, so the handover file never becomes the only record of something meant to survive the rotation.
2. Write `handover.md` in the working folder's `main-health/` directory, replacing its previous contents, under these headings (kept the same so the next thread's read is mechanical):
   - **State at handover**: `origin/main` head, the post-merge review watermark (date, run number, commit), `last-verified.txt`'s value and whether it's current or stale, and the archive issue currently in use.
   - **Routines**: the triggers' ids, cron expressions and skip conditions as actually configured, copied from the harness's trigger list, not recalled from memory.
     Note that the first daily firing of Routine 1's trigger also runs `queue-sweep`.
   - **Known failures and flakes**: anything currently red or flaky on `main`, with the tickets tracking each.
   - **In-flight and open items**: open post-merge review sub-issues, any open decision the owner hasn't answered yet.
   - **Repo PRs landed this rotation**: a one-line list of step 1's PRs, so the incoming thread can verify they're on `main` rather than taking the claim on faith.
3. Tell the incoming thread (once it exists) to disable your triggers once it confirms its own are running; don't disable them first and leave a gap where neither thread is watching.

**When you are the incoming thread**:

